  '00000000-0000-0000-0000-00000000032e',
  '00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000035',
  'quant-47-malloc-and-free',
  E'malloc and free',
  E'Memory bugs are why C has a reputation. Forget `free` and your matching engine leaks a few bytes per order — 100M orders/day × 32 bytes = 3GB/day. Free twice and it crashes mid-trading day. Read from freed memory and you might trade off stale prices and not know. Modern languages (Rust, Go, even modern C++) automate this. Production HFT shops still ship vanilla C because the overhead of even Rust''s borrow-checker is more than they''re willing to pay.',
  E'Allocate a 3-int buffer with malloc, write to it, print it, then free it.',
  E'**Concept.** `malloc(N)` returns a `void *` to N bytes of uninitialised heap memory, or `NULL` on failure. Cast the result to your pointer type. Use `sizeof(T)` so the byte count adapts to the platform''s int size. Always `free(p)` when done — every malloc must have a matching free. Production C code wraps these in arena/pool allocators for predictable latency, but the malloc/free pattern is the foundation.\n\n**Example.**\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\nint main() {\n    int *p = (int *) malloc(3 * sizeof(int));\n    p[0] = 7; p[1] = 8; p[2] = 9;\n    printf("%d %d %d\\n", p[0], p[1], p[2]);\n    free(p);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n**Your turn.** Replace `___` with the function that returns memory to the heap.\n\n**Expected.** `7 8 9`',
  null,
  null,
  '{}',
  '{"max_hint_level": 2, "do_not_reveal_solution": false, "encourage_tests_first": false}',
  array['quant', 'c-language', 'memory'],
  true,
  47
)
